{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"agent_message","text":"Reconciled the audit’s preservation findings and added Drafted identity rows:\n\n- Added `LLR-359` and `TC-398`; `docs/id-watermark` advanced `LLR 358 → 359` and `TC 397 → 398` through `trace.py --bump-ids`.\n\nRestorations applied:\n\n- SR-208 `rationale`: restored all three base sentences, including “A policy that declares a rung human-held without refusing the automated path is a claim, not a control.”\n- SR-208 `acceptance_criteria`: restored “with the rung released the change proceeds” and “a registry whose rung cannot be read is treated as held,” retaining the new state-based arms.\n- SR-208 `requirement`: repaired the one-`shall` sentence so both refusal arms are unambiguously outside the CLARITY exception.\n- LLR-246 `detail`: restored the status-reader, rung-map, committed-dial, and writer-placement sentences; restored the hook sentence with the audit’s minimal narrowed wording; restored the person/lane sentence with the audit’s minimal replacement. Kept the slot-rung retirement and narrowed marker arm.\n- TC-241 `method`: restored the DevStg-Boundary setup, released-rung, unmapped-registry, and named-writer coverage with the specified handback narrowing.\n- TC-241 `tier`: restored `tier = \"Full\"`.\n- TC-241 `evidence`: restored `tests/test_integrate_admission.py` and appended the planned held-status evidence.\n- TC-278 `method`: restored all unchanged released-rung, branch-binding, combined-act, mixed-act, and retirement sentences; left only the explicitly retired held-ledger sentences out.\n- TC-278 `expected`: restored the scope, backing, mixed-act, and retirement clauses, omitting only the retired held-ledger clause.\n- TC-278 `evidence`: restored the complete base evidence and appended planned identity/act/landing tests.\n- LLR-278 `test_refs`: restored `TC-278; TC-331` and appended the planned identity tests.\n- IF-196 `data`: restored `held_status_lines` and `held_status_refusal(dial, moves)` before the new lane-rule surfaces.\n- IF-196 `notes`: restored the base status-reader degradation explanation before the new lane-rule explanation.\n- IF-196 `requestors`: retained all base requestors and appended `scripts/text_then_act`.\n- IF-220 `notes`: restored the entire base text first, then appended the audit-only verdict sentence.\n\nNew rows:\n\n- `LLR-359`: owns the approved-text digest, per-row brief identity lines, reservation binding, outcome preservation, and pre-launch mismatch refusal.\n- `TC-398`: Drafted test case for the new identity lifecycle.\n\nOne audit-direction conflict could not be followed literally: an Approved `TC-278` cannot cite Drafted `LLR-359`; `trace.py --strict` reports that as parent-first integrity failure. I therefore kept `TC-278` on its approved parents and added Drafted `TC-398` for `LLR-359`. This preserves strict integrity.\n\nChecks:\n\n- `trace.py --strict`: passed, `integrity=0`, `orphans=0`.\n- `git diff --check`: passed.\n- Targeted IF preservation check: passed.\n- `check_trajectory.py --strict`: started but did not return before tool timeout; no pytest was run, per instruction."}}
